--TEST--
A `RuntimeError` clause takes every one of the seven classes below it, including the three whose names are namespaced, and none of the three on the other half — both sides counted rather than read off one arm — and the value it binds keeps the narrow name it was thrown under — spec § 10
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: RuntimeError
// `RuntimeError` is the half of the tree the world raises, and a program that
// writes one clause for all of it is relying on the branch being complete. What
// this pins is that completeness from both sides by counting: a class that grew
// a parent outside the branch, and one that quietly joined it, each change a
// number here while every individual line still reads plausibly.
use Core\Cli\NotInteractive;
use Core\Db\DbError;
use Core\Db\RolledBack;
use Core\Test\Failure;

final class Branch {
    public static function raise(string $kind): void {
        if ($kind == "io") { throw new IOError("io"); }
        if ($kind == "parse") { throw new ParseError("parse"); }
        if ($kind == "timeout") { throw new TimeoutError("timeout"); }
        if ($kind == "recursion") { throw new RecursionError("recursion"); }
        if ($kind == "prompt") { throw new NotInteractive("prompt"); }
        if ($kind == "rolledback") { throw new RolledBack("rolled back"); }
        throw new DbError("db");
    }

    // The four global classes and the three namespaced ones. The namespaced
    // half is the part worth counting: those rows are in the tree rather than
    // in the registry, so nothing but this says a `catch` reaches them.
    public static function taken(): uint {
        array<string> $kinds = ["io", "parse", "timeout", "recursion", "prompt", "rolledback", "db"];
        uint $taken = 0;
        foreach ($kinds as string $kind) {
            try {
                Branch::raise($kind);
            } catch (RuntimeError $theWorld) {
                $taken = $taken + 1;
            } catch (Throwable $elsewhere) {
            }
        }
        return $taken;
    }

    public static function other(string $kind): void {
        if ($kind == "logic") { throw new LogicError("logic"); }
        if ($kind == "math") { throw new ArithmeticError("math"); }
        throw new Failure("check");
    }

    // The bound from outside: the three classes that hang off the root beside
    // this branch must all miss, so a clause written for the world saying no
    // never quietly swallows a bug or a failed check.
    public static function missed(): uint {
        array<string> $kinds = ["logic", "math", "check"];
        uint $missed = 0;
        foreach ($kinds as string $kind) {
            try {
                Branch::other($kind);
            } catch (RuntimeError $wrong) {
            } catch (Throwable $right) {
                $missed = $missed + 1;
            }
        }
        return $missed;
    }

    // Catching wide does not make the value wide: what the clause binds is
    // still the class that was thrown, so a handler can widen its `catch` and
    // narrow again inside it.
    public static function keepsItsName(): string {
        try {
            throw new IOError("the report file went away");
        } catch (RuntimeError $caught) {
            if ($caught is IOError) {
                return "still an IOError";
            }
            return "widened to " . $caught->message;
        }
    }
}

echo Branch::taken(), "\n";
echo Branch::missed(), "\n";
echo Branch::keepsItsName(), "\n";
--EXPECT--
7
3
still an IOError
